# What this file is worth here, and what it is not.
#
# The Dockerfile copies NOTHING out of the build context: Claude Code comes from
# npm, its runtime dependencies from apt, and not one line of this repository
# ends up in the image. So every path listed below is sent to the Docker daemon,
# hashed into the context, and then never read. This repository is a handful of
# text files, so what this saves is close to nothing -- which is exactly why the
# file has to be argued rather than assumed.
#
# It is kept for three reasons that do not depend on how big the context is.
#
#   - The context is what a build is ALLOWED to see. The day someone adds a
#     "COPY . ." -- by hand, or from a snippet that assumes an application image
#     -- the difference between this file existing and not existing is the whole
#     repository silently landing in a published layer, agent configuration and
#     all. Nothing warns about that, and a layer that has shipped is public.
#   - ".git" is the one entry with a real cost. It grows with every commit while
#     the working tree stays the same size, and it is sent in full on every local
#     build.
#   - It states the intent. "This image is built from npm and apt, not from this
#     tree" is a design decision, and a reader who wants to check it finds it
#     written down instead of inferred from the absence of a COPY.
#
# Excluding everything with a bare "*" would be the shortest way to say the same
# thing, and was considered. It is not used because it breaks the next COPY
# instead of governing it: a build that suddenly cannot find a file it just added
# reads as a Docker bug, while an explicit list reads as a decision to amend.

# Version control history. The heaviest thing in the context, and the only entry
# whose cost grows over time. Nothing in the build reads it.
.git
.gitattributes
.gitignore

# Repository automation. The build workflow lives in here and DRIVES the build;
# it is never an input to it.
.github

# Instructions and pre-approved permissions for Claude Code sessions working ON
# this repository. They describe the repository, not the image, and the image is
# emphatically not the place to ship a permission list.
.claude
CLAUDE.md

# Documentation. Written for a reader on GitHub, and reachable from inside the
# image only if someone puts it there on purpose.
README.md

# Legal annexes -- LICENSE today, anything alongside it later. The image carries
# no file from this repository, so the AGPL text that covers the Dockerfile and
# the workflow has no reader inside the container; what the image does ship is
# Node, the Debian packages and the npm package, each under its own terms and
# each carrying its own licence where it was installed. Keeping LICENSE out of
# the CONTEXT changes nothing about how this repository is licensed or
# distributed.
LICENSE*
NOTICE
